Looking at this problem, I need to find $v_{2011}(p_{2012} - p_{2011})$ where $p_1 = 2012$ and $p_n = 2012^{p_{n-1}}$.

## Setup

Let $q = 2011$ (prime) and $A = 2012 = q + 1$. Then $p_n = A^{p_{n-1}}$ with $p_1 = A$.

Define $f(n) = v_q(p_n - p_{n-1})$ for $n \geq 2$.

## Establishing the Recurrence ($n \geq 3$)

$$p_n - p_{n-1} = A^{p_{n-1}} - A^{p_{n-2}} = A^{p_{n-2}}\!\left(A^{p_{n-1}-p_{n-2}} - 1\right)$$

Since $\gcd(A, q) = \gcd(q+1, q) = 1$, we have $v_q(A^{p_{n-2}}) = 0$, so:

$$f(n) = v_q\!\left(A^{p_{n-1}-p_{n-2}} - 1\right)$$

Now $A \equiv 1 \pmod{q}$, so $q \mid A - 1$. By the **Lifting the Exponent Lemma** (for odd prime $q$ with $q \mid A-1$ and $q \nmid A$):

$$v_q(A^N - 1) = v_q(A-1) + v_q(N)$$

Here $A - 1 = q$ so $v_q(A-1) = 1$, and $N = p_{n-1} - p_{n-2}$. Therefore:

$$f(n) = 1 + v_q(p_{n-1} - p_{n-2}) = 1 + f(n-1) \quad \text{for all } n \geq 3$$

## Base Case ($n = 2$)

$$f(2) = v_q(p_2 - p_1) = v_q(A^A - A) = v_q\!\left(A\left(A^{A-1} - 1\right)\right) = v_q(A^{A-1} - 1)$$

Applying LTE with exponent $N = A - 1 = q$:

$$f(2) = v_q(A - 1) + v_q(A - 1) = 1 + v_q(q) = 1 + 1 = 2$$

## Solving the Recurrence

With $f(2) = 2$ and $f(n) = 1 + f(n-1)$:

$$f(n) = n \quad \text{for all } n \geq 2$$

## Verification with Small Example

Using $q = 3$, $A = 4$: $p_1 = 4$, $p_2 = 256$, $p_3 = 4^{256}$.

- $f(2) = v_3(252) = v_3(4 \cdot 63) = v_3(9 \cdot 7) = 2$ ✓
- $f(3) = v_3(4^{256} - 256) = v_3(4^4(4^{252}-1)) = v_3(4^{252}-1) = 1 + v_3(252) = 1 + 2 = 3$ ✓

## Conclusion

$$k = f(2012) = 2012$$

The largest integer $k$ such that $p_{2012} - p_{2011}$ is divisible by $2011^k$ is $\boxed{2012}$.

